{
  "id": "database",
  "title": "Index Fund",
  "coach": {
    "board": "The database and its schema on one side, the queries (or migration files) on the other, with how long each takes.",
    "actions": "Next day lets traffic run and bills you for what is still slow. The terminal changes the schema.",
    "firstMove": "Press EXPLAIN ANALYZE on the slowest query and look for a Seq Scan in the plan."
  },
  "healthLabel": "Error budget",
  "tickLabel": "Day",
  "manual": true,
  "stacked": true,
  "seed": 11,
  "events": [
    {
      "id": "login-seq-scan",
      "title": "Logins are crawling",
      "fix": "EXPLAIN shows a Seq Scan on users: the planner reads every row to find one email. A UNIQUE index on users (email) turns that into an Index Scan and also enforces one account per email.",
      "learn": {
        "note": "topics/database/notes/03-queries-and-indexing.md",
        "section": "Why an index"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "fk-unindexed",
      "title": "The order history page is slow",
      "fix": "orders.user_id is a foreign key, but Postgres does not index foreign keys for you, so WHERE user_id = ... is a Seq Scan. Index the column you filter and join on.",
      "learn": {
        "note": "topics/database/notes/03-queries-and-indexing.md",
        "section": "What to index"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sort-after-index",
      "title": "Recent orders for a user is still slowish",
      "fix": "The user_id index finds the rows, then Postgres sorts them. An index on (user_id, created_at) hands them back already in order, so ORDER BY ... LIMIT 10 reads ten entries. Column order matters: (created_at, user_id) would not help.",
      "learn": {
        "note": "topics/database/notes/03-queries-and-indexing.md",
        "section": "What to index"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "product-seq-scan",
      "title": "\"Who bought this product\" is scanning order_items",
      "fix": "The primary key is (order_id, product_id): it serves lookups by order_id, not by product_id alone. A filter on the second column needs its own index.",
      "learn": {
        "note": "topics/database/notes/03-queries-and-indexing.md",
        "section": "What to index"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n-plus-one",
      "title": "The order page runs one query per line item",
      "fix": "One query for the items, then one per item for its product: 1 + N round trips. Fetch it with a single JOIN and deploy that statement.",
      "learn": {
        "note": "topics/database/notes/03-queries-and-indexing.md",
        "section": "The N+1 query problem"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "over-indexed",
      "title": "Writes are slow or the disk is filling up",
      "fix": "Every index is updated on every INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE and takes storage. Look at SELECT * FROM pg_stat_user_indexes, and drop indexes whose idx_scan stays at 0.",
      "learn": {
        "note": "topics/database/notes/03-queries-and-indexing.md",
        "section": "What to index"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "report-seq-scan",
      "title": "Finance asks for an index on orders.status",
      "fix": "status = 'shipped' matches most of the table, so the planner correctly reads the table instead. An index pays off when a filter keeps a small share of the rows; check EXPLAIN first, and drop an index that does not help.",
      "learn": {
        "note": "topics/database/notes/03-queries-and-indexing.md",
        "section": "When an index does not help"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "traffic-growth",
      "title": "Traffic grew: the table is much bigger",
      "fix": "A seq scan costs time in proportion to the rows, so a query that was fine at 20,000 rows is slow at 300,000. An index lookup grows with the log of the rows.",
      "learn": {
        "note": "topics/database/notes/03-queries-and-indexing.md",
        "section": "Why an index"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "order-page-launch",
      "title": "Marketing launches the order page",
      "fix": "A new page ships with its own queries. Run EXPLAIN on each before traffic arrives, and count how many queries one request makes.",
      "learn": {
        "note": "topics/database/notes/03-queries-and-indexing.md",
        "section": "EXPLAIN"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "release-add-column",
      "title": "Release 1: orders.currency (migration 0006, app v2)",
      "fix": "An additive change: run the migration first, then roll out the app that uses the column. During a rolling deploy old and new app versions run side by side, so the schema must work for both.",
      "learn": {
        "note": "topics/database/notes/02-migrations.md",
        "section": "In CI/CD"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "deploy-before-migrate",
      "title": "The app started before its migration ran",
      "fix": "The new app reads a column the schema does not have yet. Migrations run as a separate step before the new application version starts.",
      "learn": {
        "note": "topics/database/notes/02-migrations.md",
        "section": "In CI/CD"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "release-new-column",
      "title": "Release 2: orders.shipped_at (migration 0007, app v3)",
      "fix": "Test the pair in a throwaway database before production: up, down, up. A down that does not undo its up is otherwise found at rollback time, the worst moment.",
      "learn": {
        "note": "topics/database/notes/02-migrations.md",
        "section": "up/down"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "broken-down",
      "title": "A down migration did not undo its up",
      "fix": "The down file left the column behind, so the next up fails with \"already exists\" and a rollback leaves a schema nobody can reproduce. Write the down, then run up, down, up in staging.",
      "learn": {
        "note": "topics/database/notes/02-migrations.md",
        "section": "up/down"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "bad-deploy",
      "title": "App v3 is failing in production",
      "fix": "Roll back: put the previous app version back, then migrate down 1. A working down file is what makes a rollback one command instead of an improvised ALTER TABLE.",
      "learn": {
        "note": "topics/database/notes/02-migrations.md",
        "section": "Running them"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "release-drop-column",
      "title": "Release 3: drop users.legacy_username (migration 0008, app v4)",
      "fix": "A destructive change: deploy the app that stops reading the column first, and drop the column afterwards. Dropping it in the same step as the code that stops using it leaves a window where the old code is still running.",
      "learn": {
        "note": "topics/database/notes/02-migrations.md",
        "section": "In CI/CD"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "drop-in-use",
      "title": "A column disappeared while the running app still reads it",
      "fix": "The old app version was still live when the migration ran. \"Drop a column\" needs the code to stop using it in an earlier release. Rolling back with a down puts the column back, but not its data.",
      "learn": {
        "note": "topics/database/notes/02-migrations.md",
        "section": "In CI/CD"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "edited-applied",
      "title": "A teammate edited migration 0003, which has already run",
      "fix": "An applied migration is history: production never re-runs 0003, so the edit would only reach fresh databases. Restore the file and put the change in a new numbered migration.",
      "learn": {
        "note": "topics/database/notes/02-migrations.md",
        "section": "Never edit an applied migration"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "schema-drift",
      "title": "Production no longer matches its migrations",
      "fix": "Someone changed the schema by hand. A fresh database built from the migrations would differ from production. Make every change a migration so each environment gets the same one.",
      "learn": {
        "note": "topics/database/notes/02-migrations.md",
        "section": "Why not just hand-edit the schema"
      }
    }
  ],
